    <!-- k4: the four status bar dimens below are the stock A52s pixel values (36, 33, 87 and 72 px on the 1080 px
         wide, 409.432 dpi panel) written in mm, as Samsung does on its multi-resolution phones. Pixels do not follow
         Display > Screen resolution (HD+ / 540p), so the status bar became 1.5x / 2x too tall and its contents moved
         in. mm follows the display's physical dpi, which patches/resolution_scaling scales with a forced size, and
         unlike dp it ignores Screen zoom, exactly like the stock px values. +0.02 px so both rounding
         (getDimensionPixelSize) and truncation (getDimensionPixelOffset) give the stock pixel count at FHD+. -->
    <dimen name="status_bar_camera_side_padding">2.2346mm</dimen>
    <dimen name="status_bar_camera_top_margin">2.0485mm</dimen>
    <dimen name="status_bar_height_portrait">5.3985mm</dimen>
    <dimen name="status_bar_indicator_corner_padding">4.4679mm</dimen>
    <!-- k4: SystemUI IndicatorScaleGardener.getLatestScaleModel() takes this ratio as is when it is >= 0.75 and then
         never calls getMaxRatio(), which reads IndicatorGardenPresenter.cachedGardenModel. That model is half updated
         while a resolution switch (Display > Screen resolution) is delivered - new status bar height, camera top
         margin of the previous resolution - and callers that sample the ratio at that moment keep a wrong one until
         SystemUI restarts: the status bar notification icons were 20 % smaller at HD+ / 540p (ratio clamped to 0.75)
         and 9 % larger after switching back to FHD+ (0.934) than after a boot. 0.8588 = (88 px status bar - 33 px
         camera top margin) / 68 px (24dp) + 0.05, the value the A52s settles on at FHD+ (see
         patches/statusbar_icon_size), so clock, battery and status icons keep their FHD+ size; HD+ (was 0.872) and
         540p (was 0.844) become identical to it. Side effect: the ratio no longer rises in landscape. -->
    <dimen name="status_bar_scale_garden_fixed_ratio">0.8588</dimen>
</resources>
